var e=`<p>These two acronyms appear in every brochure and are regularly presented as though they were the same thing, or as though either one meant a degree was recognized. Neither is true.</p>
<h2>NAAC is an assessment, not a permission</h2>
<p>The National Assessment and Accreditation Council evaluates an institution against criteria covering teaching, research, infrastructure, governance and student support, and awards a grade on a scale running to A++. The assessment is voluntary, it is repeated in cycles, and the grade carries the cycle and the year it was awarded.</p>
<p>What it tells you is that an external body examined the institution and formed a view. What it does not tell you is whether a specific programme may be offered at a distance, which is an entirely separate entitlement.</p>
<p>A grade without its cycle and year is close to meaningless. Ask for both.</p>
<h2>NIRF is a comparative rank</h2>
<p>The National Institutional Ranking Framework ranks institutions annually within categories, using teaching and learning resources, research, graduation outcomes, outreach and perception. It is a position relative to other institutions in a given year, and positions move.</p>
<p>A rank is most useful for comparing institutions in the same category in the same year. It is least useful as a permanent attribute of a university, which is how brochures usually present it.</p>
<h2>When these should carry weight</h2>
<p>They matter when your plan depends on how the institution is perceived: applying to a foreign university later, a competitive employer that screens on institutional reputation, or an academic career.</p>
<p>They matter much less when you need a recognized qualification to satisfy a promotion criterion or a government eligibility rule. Those ask whether the degree is recognized, not where the university ranks.</p>
<h2>What actually decides whether the degree counts</h2>
<p>Entitlement for the programme, in the mode you are studying, in the session you enrolled. A high NAAC grade on a university that is not entitled to run your programme online does not make that degree valid, and an unranked university that is properly entitled awards a degree that is.</p>
<p>Check recognition first. Use accreditation and ranking to choose between the options that survive that check.</p>
<h2>If a claim looks inflated</h2>
<p>Ask for the grade with its cycle and year, and the rank with its category and year, and check both against the awarding body&#39;s own published lists. Send them to us if you want them verified. We would rather tell you a claim does not hold up than have you find out after the fee is paid.</p>
`;export{e as html};